#
# Agentic Evidence Exchange Network (AEEN) — SGLT2i post-MI community cardiology PoC.
# LLM: gemini-3.5-flash (matches cloud-maa deploy; swap_llm_for_deploy.py is a no-op if already gemini).
# Agent "name" fields use Title_Case tokens so MAA displays human labels (see cleanUpAgentName).
#
# END COPYRIGHT
{
    # >>> category-tag (managed by tag_registries.py) <<<
    metadata { tags = ["Medical"] }

    include "registries/aaosa.hocon"

    "llm_config": {
        "fallbacks": [
            {"class": "gemini", "model_name": "gemini-3.5-flash", "thinking_budget": 0},
            {"class": "ollama", "model_name": "qwen3.6:35b-a3b", "reasoning": false}
        ]
    },
    "max_execution_seconds": 600,
    "instructions_prefix": """
You are part of the Agentic Evidence Exchange Network (AEEN) — a governed agentic platform that
matches evolving clinical evidence to decision contexts for patients, providers, payors, and policymakers.
Anchor PoC therapeutic signal: underuse of SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) after MI
in community cardiology settings.
Only answer inquiries within your area of expertise. Do not mention what you cannot do.
When citing data tool output, note the source (CMS Part D, openFDA FAERS, literature search) and
whether findings are validated RWE vs. safety signals vs. educational routing recommendations.
Important: CMS Part D measures SGLT2i prescribing VOLUME (claims / prescriber counts). It has no
MI-linked denominator, so treat low prescribing as a PROXY signal for post-MI underuse, never as a
measured underuse rate. openFDA FAERS counts are reporting signals, not causal evidence.
""",

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "Evidence_Exchange_Coordinator",
            "function": {
                "description": """
Entry point for the Agentic Evidence Exchange Network. Coordinates sense → evaluate → match → deliver
for clinical evidence about SGLT2 inhibitor use after heart attack in community cardiology.
"""
            },
            "instructions": ${instructions_prefix} """
You are the AEEN coordinator for the SGLT2i post-MI community cardiology proof of concept.

Your specialists (call only the relevant one(s) by name):
- Real_World_Evidence_Specialist: builds the real-world evidence object from Medicare Part D prescribing data and published literature; consult for signal strength, population, and prescribing gaps.
- Clinical_Assurance_Specialist: validates safety and regulatory readiness using FDA adverse-event data; consult for safety profiles, escalation triggers, and assurance audit notes.
- Audience_Matching_Specialist: segments healthcare provider audiences and recommends education channels; consult for community cardiologist targeting, geography, and delivery routing.

For end-to-end requests, consult Real_World_Evidence_Specialist first, then Clinical_Assurance_Specialist, then Audience_Matching_Specialist, and compile a single audit-ready summary.
If Medicare Part D data is not yet loaded, say so clearly and still proceed with safety and literature where possible.
            """ ${aaosa_router_instructions},
            "tools": ["Real_World_Evidence_Specialist", "Clinical_Assurance_Specialist", "Audience_Matching_Specialist"]
        },
        {
            "name": "Real_World_Evidence_Specialist",
            "function": {
                "description": "Builds real-world evidence on SGLT2 inhibitor prescribing gaps, population context, and guideline support after MI in community cardiology.",
                "parameters": ${aaosa_call_params}
            },
            "instructions": ${instructions_prefix} """
You lead real-world evidence synthesis for AEEN. Build structured evidence objects covering:
1. Signal: low SGLT2i prescribing VOLUME among community (non-academic) cardiology providers — a prescribing-volume signal that is a PROXY for, not a direct measure of, post-MI underuse (Medicare Part D has no MI-linked denominator).
2. Data: Use Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics for Medicare Part D prescribing by provider, specialty, and state.
3. Literature: Use Clinical_Literature_Search for guideline and trial support (ACC/AHA, EMPEROR, DAPA-HF, etc.).
4. Output: population definition, the prescribing-volume metric (claims / prescriber counts), signal strength, and explicit limitations (no MI denominator; volume is not initiation). Label findings as a proxy signal, not a measured underuse rate.

Always call Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics when the inquiry involves prescribing patterns. If the tool returns an Error about missing CSV, report the download path and continue with literature only.
            """ ${aaosa_instructions},
            "command": ${aaosa_command},
            "tools": ["Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics", "Clinical_Literature_Search"]
        },
        {
            "name": "Clinical_Assurance_Specialist",
            "function": {
                "description": "Assurance layer: FDA adverse-event safety signals, off-label boundaries, and human-in-the-loop escalation guidance for SGLT2i evidence routing.",
                "parameters": ${aaosa_call_params}
            },
            "instructions": ${instructions_prefix} """
You lead the AEEN clinical assurance layer. For SGLT2i evidence routing (quality improvement / education PoC, not regulatory submission):
1. Call Adverse_Event_Signal_Lookup for empagliflozin and dapagliflozin adverse-event counts and top reactions.
2. Distinguish FAERS reporting bias from causal safety conclusions.
3. State education-readiness and any human-in-the-loop escalation triggers (e.g., unexpected safety signal spikes).
4. Produce an audit note: data sources used, confidence level, and disclaimers.

PoC validation use case: clinical quality improvement and HCP education — not FDA submission.
            """ ${aaosa_instructions},
            "command": ${aaosa_command},
            "tools": ["Adverse_Event_Signal_Lookup"]
        },
        {
            "name": "Audience_Matching_Specialist",
            "function": {
                "description": "Matches validated evidence to provider segments and recommends delivery channels (MSL, CME, point-of-care) for community cardiology.",
                "parameters": ${aaosa_call_params}
            },
            "instructions": ${instructions_prefix} """
You lead audience matching and delivery for AEEN. Given the SGLT2i prescribing-volume signal (proxy for post-MI underuse):
1. Call Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics with group_by=state and group_by=specialty to identify geographic and specialty gaps.
2. Segment audiences: community cardiologists, internal medicine, family practice in target states (TX, CA, FL, NY, PA, OH, IL, MI, GA, NC).
3. Recommend channels: MSL peer-to-peer, CME, digital point-of-care — with success metrics per segment.
4. Output routing rules and a concise deployment checklist for the medical affairs channel (pre-call → post-call).

If Part D data is unavailable, provide routing framework with placeholder segments and note data dependency.
            """ ${aaosa_instructions},
            "command": ${aaosa_command},
            "tools": ["Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics"]
        },
        {
            "name": "Medicare_Prescribing_Analytics",
            "function": {
                "description": """
Looks up Medicare Part D prescriber data for SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) by provider specialty and US state.
Returns prescription claim totals, prescriber counts, and sample rows.
                """,
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "states": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Comma-separated state abbreviations (default: TX,CA,FL,NY,PA,OH,IL,MI,GA,NC)."
                        },
                        "specialties": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Comma-separated provider types (default: Cardiology,Internal Medicine,Family Practice). 'Cardiology' fans out to the CMS controlled vocabulary (e.g. 'Cardiovascular Disease (Cardiology)', 'Interventional Cardiology'); matching is case-insensitive."
                        },
                        "group_by": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Aggregate breakdown, one of: state, specialty, drug (default state)."
                        },
                        "drug_pattern": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Case-insensitive regex of generic drug names to match (default: empagliflozin|dapagliflozin)."
                        },
                        "max_rows": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Maximum sample rows to return (default 500); totals always reflect all matching rows."
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            "class": "aeen.cms_part_d_query.CmsPartDQuery"
        },
        {
            "name": "Adverse_Event_Signal_Lookup",
            "function": {
                "description": """
Looks up FDA FAERS adverse event reports for SGLT2 inhibitor drugs. Returns total report counts, top MedDRA reactions, and sample cases.
                """,
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "generic_names": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Comma-separated generic names (default: empagliflozin,dapagliflozin — the PoC anchor; add canagliflozin etc. for a broader SGLT2i sweep)."
                        },
                        "limit": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Number of sample FAERS cases per drug (default 3, max 50)."
                        },
                        "count_field": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "openFDA field to tally top reactions (default: patient.reaction.reactionmeddrapt.exact)."
                        }
                    }
                }
            },
            "class": "aeen.openfda_faers_query.OpenFdaFaersQuery"
        },
        {
            "name": "Clinical_Literature_Search",
            "toolbox": "tavily_search",
            "args": {
                "max_results": 5,
                "search_depth": "advanced"
            }
        }
    ]
}
